Relevant columns for the requested forecast task  

monthly_sales.product_id : historical sales series that we have to model/forecast.  
monthly_sales.mth        : month (text ISO‐date) – will be used to derive time‑index, year, seasonality, etc.  
monthly_sales.qty        : observed sales quantity – dependent variable for the regression / forecast.  

Optional helper columns (not strictly needed for the core forecast, but sometimes handy)  

products.id              : join key if you want a readable product name.  
products.name            : a readable label when inspecting the two products.  

Typical conditions you will apply  

WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)                     -- restrict to the 2 items  
AND   mth >= '2016‑01‑01'                            -- first month of the 36‑month history  
AND   mth <  '2019‑01‑01'                            -- stop after 36 months (Jan‑2016 … Dec‑2018)  

Below are ten exploratory SQL snippets (all SELECT only, each different, all limited to 20 rows) that help you look at the raw data step‑by‑step before you perform the weighted‑regression forecast.

```sql
-- 1. List all distinct product_id values that have monthly sales data
SELECT DISTINCT product_id
FROM monthly_sales
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Peek at the first 20 month / qty rows for product 4160
SELECT mth, qty
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id = 4160
ORDER BY mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Peek at the first 20 month / qty rows for product 7790
SELECT mth, qty
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id = 7790
ORDER BY mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Show sales for both products in one result set, restricted to the
--    36‑month history window (Jan‑2016 – Dec‑2018)
SELECT product_id, mth, qty
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
  AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
ORDER BY product_id, mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Display the yearly totals (sums) for each product inside the 36‑month window
SELECT product_id,
       substr(mth, 1, 4) AS year,
       SUM(qty)         AS yearly_qty
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
  AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
GROUP BY product_id, substr(mth, 1, 4)
ORDER BY product_id, year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Compute the average monthly sales for each product in the historic window
SELECT product_id,
       AVG(qty) AS avg_monthly_qty
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
  AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
GROUP BY product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Create a time index (1 … 36) you can later feed into a regression
SELECT product_id,
       mth,
       qty,
       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY mth) AS time_idx
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
  AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
ORDER BY product_id, mth
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Flag the seasonality window (time_idx 7–30) that will get extra weight
WITH hist AS (
  SELECT product_id,
         mth,
         qty,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY mth) AS time_idx
  FROM monthly_sales
  WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
    AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
    AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
)
SELECT product_id,
       mth,
       qty,
       time_idx,
       CASE
         WHEN time_idx BETWEEN 7 AND 30 THEN 2    -- give weight 2 to those points
         ELSE 1
       END AS weight_flag
FROM hist
ORDER BY product_id, time_idx
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Verify that every product really has exactly 36 months of data
SELECT product_id,
       COUNT(*) AS row_cnt
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
  AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
GROUP BY product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. (Data sanity) Look for months with zero or missing sales, which might
--     influence a regression forecast
SELECT product_id, mth, qty
FROM monthly_sales
WHERE product_id IN (4160, 7790)
  AND mth >= '2016-01-01'
  AND mth <  '2019-01-01'
  AND (qty IS NULL OR qty = 0)
ORDER BY product_id, mth
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries give you all the information needed to feed a weighted regression that:

1. Uses the first 36 months of data.  
2. Applies higher weights to observations from time steps 7 … 30 to handle the seasonality pattern.  
3. Generates a forecast for the 12 calendar months of 2018.  

From that forecast, simply add the twelve monthly predictions for each product and divide by 12 to get the average annual sales per product for 2018, and then average the two products if you need one consolidated figure.
